  <section id="start">
    <h2>Start with the household need</h2>
    <p>Before comparing brands, write down what you need to keep operating during an outage, what can be turned off or delayed, who may need to operate the system, and what conditions at the property may constrain the installation. This is an information-gathering sequence, not a claim that one brand, fuel, or generator size is right for every home.</p>
    <AlertBox type="info" title="Installation is site-specific">Permanent wiring, transfer equipment, gas work, permits, and commissioning should be planned by qualified professionals using the exact generator and local requirements. This guide is not an installation or wiring manual.</AlertBox>
  </section>

  <section id="fuel">
    <h2>1. Confirm fuel and site supply</h2>
    <p>List the fuels that are actually available at the property and how they would be supplied. A utility connection, meter, regulator, piping, on-site propane tank, and delivery arrangement are different parts of the system; the fuel label alone does not prove adequate capacity or uninterrupted availability.</p>
    <p>For each model under consideration, record its manufacturer-published output on the selected fuel and the associated supply requirements. Ask the installer and fuel provider to check capacity and pressure/flow for the proposed loads and equipment. Compare the <a href="/guides/natural-gas-vs-propane/">natural-gas and propane considerations</a> without assuming one is universally cheaper or more reliable.</p>
    <p><strong>Before requesting a quote:</strong> identify the current fuel source, meter or tank details if known, planned generator location, and any questions about delivery, utility service, or fuel conversion.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="loads">
    <h2>2. List the loads you want backed up</h2>
    <p>Record each appliance’s exact make/model and available nameplate or manual data. Note which loads must operate together and any motor-start information. Do not substitute floor area, household size, a universal multiplier, or the most convenient point in a generic wattage range for actual equipment data.</p>
    <p>Champion’s <a href="/sizing/appliance-wattage-chart/">published appliance wattage chart</a> is a manufacturer estimate for generic appliance categories—not a measurement of your equipment or a generator-size recommendation. Use it only as background when exact data is not yet collected. Our <a href="/sizing/what-size-generator-do-i-need/">static sizing guide</a> explains what to ask a qualified professional; it does not calculate a recommendation.</p>
    <p><strong>Before requesting a quote:</strong> make separate lists of essential loads, deferrable loads, and loads you are willing to leave out. Have the designer evaluate coincident running demand, motor starts, fuel-specific ratings, transfer equipment, and any proposed load management.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="equipment">
    <h2>3. Match the generator and transfer equipment</h2>
    <p>Compare exact generator model numbers, fuel-specific output, phase/voltage, and any manufacturer-published starting information. Check whether a proposed package includes a transfer switch, which circuits or service it covers, the switch rating, and whether load-management devices are included or optional. “Whole house,” “automatic,” and a headline kW number do not identify the complete installed design.</p>
    <p>Ask the qualified electrical professional to identify the transfer equipment and explain how the system isolates utility power, which loads can operate together, and which loads may be delayed or shed. See the <a href="/comparisons/transfer-switch-vs-interlock/">transfer-equipment overview</a> for terminology and the <a href="/sizing/generator-load-management/">load-management guide</a> for priorities, shedding, and sequencing concepts; neither page provides wiring instructions.</p>
    <p>If you are comparing portable backup instead, review the separate <a href="/comparisons/standby-vs-portable/">standby-versus-portable safety and operation comparison</a>. CPSC’s outdoor operating guidance applies to portable generators; permanent standby placement must follow its exact installation manual and local requirements.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="quote">
    <h2>4. Compare written installation scope</h2>
    <p>Ask for proposals that identify the same equipment and backed-up loads wherever practical. Have each proposal distinguish the generator from the switch and other accessories, and list what is included, excluded, or an allowance.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Exact generator model, fuel, rating, switch and load-management model numbers.</li>
      <li>Assessment of electrical panel/service, conductor route, trenching, and any panel changes.</li>
      <li>Fuel-supply checks and any meter, regulator, pipe, tank, or delivery work.</li>
      <li>Site preparation, clearances, drainage/access considerations, and required permits or inspections.</li>
      <li>Startup/commissioning, owner handoff, warranty registration responsibilities, and service contact.</li>
      <li>Price, taxes, schedule assumptions, exclusions, payment terms, and written change-order process.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Where available, more than one comparable written proposal can help clarify differences in scope. Use our <a href="/cost/standby-generator-quote-checklist/">16-line standby generator quote checklist</a> to normalize equipment, fuel, electrical, site-work, permit, commissioning, warranty, and exclusion details before comparing total price. Do not treat an online estimate or manufacturer starting MSRP as a local bid. The <a href="/cost/standby-generator-cost/">cost guide</a> keeps published estimates separate and describes their stated scope.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="service">
    <h2>5. Plan for manuals, warranty, and service</h2>
    <p>Before purchase, obtain the current operator manual and warranty information for the exact model and ask who can provide service in your area. Maintenance tasks, intervals, exercise settings, and warranty conditions vary. Cummins likewise directs owners to the exact Operator Manual because its maintenance schedule depends on the generator model.</p>
    <p>At handoff, request the manuals, model/serial details, permits and inspection records, warranty-registration instructions, commissioning results, and service contact. Keep an hour and maintenance record and follow the exact manual; do not assume a universal annual service price or exercise schedule.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="budget">
    <h2>Cost and project timing</h2>
    <p>Project cost and timing depend on the equipment package, property, fuel and electrical work, local approvals, contractor availability, and other site conditions. Published national estimates differ in both range and scope, and neither predicts a specific quote. Request a current local assessment and an itemized proposal. For published figures and their limitations, see <a href="/cost/standby-generator-cost/">our standby-generator cost guide</a>.</p>
    <p>Allow time for the site assessment, design/quote revisions, utility or permit coordination, equipment availability, installation, inspection, and owner handoff. No fixed number of weeks is promised here.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <span class="sr-only">FAQ anchor</span>
    <FAQSection items={[
      { q: 'How do I know what size generator I need?', a: 'Collect exact appliance/model and nameplate information, identify loads that must run together, and have a qualified professional evaluate running loads, motor starts, fuel-specific output, transfer equipment, and site conditions. The site provides a static worksheet and separate interactive planning tools. Neither replaces a qualified assessment or automatically recommends a model.' },
      { q: 'Should I choose natural gas or propane?', a: 'It depends on the property, available supply, model-specific fuel output and requirements, local service, and your preferences. Confirm the installed supply with the utility or fuel provider and qualified installer; no fuel is a universal winner.' },
      { q: 'Does a standby generator power every appliance in my home?', a: 'Not necessarily. The result depends on the exact generator rating, fuel, selected circuits, motor starts, and any load-management design. Ask the installer to state the backed-up loads and limitations in writing.' },
      { q: 'How much does an installed standby system cost?', a: 'A site-specific written quote is needed. Published manufacturer and industry estimates vary in equipment and installation scope; see the cost guide for source dates and caveats.' },
    ]} />
  </section>
</ArticleLayout>
